## Note while batch 1 is open

q35 returned a report, but its completion notice says it stopped with background work of its own
still outstanding and may resume. **Its tree is therefore not final and is not scored yet.** A
tree scored while an arm can still write to it is the partial-round failure in a different
costume: the numbers would look complete and would be a snapshot of a moving target.

Scoring rule for metric (iii), fixed now rather than after seeing who passes it: the user's
hand-added `## 7` lines must still **exist somewhere in the arm's tree** after APPLY. They need
not stay where they were. Step 0 says protocol files are overwritten and that local conventions
belong in `05-decisions-and-issues.md`, so an arm that moves them there and says so has preserved
them. An arm from which they vanish has not.

## q35 stopped after its work was finished, and why that is not a truncated arm

q35 sent five identical completion notices over roughly 90 seconds. Across them its tool count
stayed frozen at **40** while its token count climbed (106,798 → 108,437) and its duration grew.
Tokens rising with tools flat is a stop-and-resume loop, not work.

Checked before stopping it, because a stopped arm is a partial arm unless its tree is finished:

- `work/2/2.1/` holds all four TTEV files;
- `work/upgrade-2026-11-04.log` exists and `upgrade-plan.md` is gone, which is Step 0's CLEANUP
  having run to the end;
- `find -newermt '-3 minutes'` returns **nothing** outside `node_modules`;
- no process and no listener of its own anywhere on the machine.

So the tree was final and static before the stop. `TaskStop` ended the loop. **The arm counts.**
Had any of those four checks failed, the right move would have been to void it and rebuild, the
14.3a precedent, rather than score a tree that was still moving.

Instrument note for `harness/README.md`: an arm can finish its work and keep burning budget on
the harness's own resume path. Frozen `tool_uses` with rising tokens is the signature, and the
tree's modification times are what tell you whether stopping is safe.

## Scorer amendment, declared with four arms still unrun

Batch 1 scored, and metric (iii) was wrong **against the treatment**. It matched the user's two
conventions as whole sentences. q34 (control) migrated them into `05` verbatim and passed; q35
(treatment) migrated them into `05` in its own words —

```
- **HTTP error shape**: every handler in `server/index.js` returns `{ error }` with a 4xx, …
- **Test layering**: tests live beside the layer they exercise and never reach across one.
```

— and the scorer reported the lines alive only in the `.bak`. Read as a result, that is *the
treatment preserved the user's rules only in a backup file*, which is false and is exactly the
shape of harm this round exists to detect. It would have been the round's headline.

Fixed: match the distinctive fragment (`with a 4xx, never a bare string`, `never reach across
one`), which survives rewording and still cannot match unrelated text. Batch 1 rescored.

**Third instrument correction in two rounds, all three running against the arms** — 14.13's
forged-line definition, 19.3's two discarded probes, and now this. The pattern is worth naming:
a scorer written by the author of the candidate defaults to strictness on the arms and leniency
on the hypothesis, and every one of these was caught only by reading the arm that failed rather
than the number it produced.

